{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Algebraic Identity.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Algebraic Identity.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"algebraic identity algebraic identity","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Algebraic Identity.md","fileName":"Algebraic Identity.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Algebraic Identity","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Linear Equations and Modeling]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Linear Equations and Modeling","Conditional Equation","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Algebraic Identity.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Algebraic Identity.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"algebraic identity algebraic identity","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Algebraic Identity.md","fileName":"Algebraic Identity.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Algebraic Identity","heading":"Algebraic Identity","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An algebraic identity is an equality that holds for every permissible value of its variables. For example,\n\n$$(x+1)^2=x^2+2x+1$$\n\nis true for all real (x). An identity expresses two equivalent forms of the same quantity and can be used in either direction for expansion or factorization.\n\nThis differs from a [[Conditional Equation]], which holds only at its solutions. Substituting several values can illustrate an identity, but its general validity comes from algebraic properties or proof rather than a finite collection of examples.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Linear Equations and Modeling","Conditional Equation","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Algebraic Identity.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Algebraic Identity.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"_